The  driver  supports  the EA41x bus interface.  This driver is a
bit unusual.  It must look like a network interface and  it  must
also appear to be a device to the system.  In addition, to facil‐
itate  commands  issued by userland programs, there are and entry
points.  This allows a user program to, for example, display  the
EA41x  statistic  and  download new code into the adaptor - func‐
tions which can’t be performed through the interface.  Normal op‐
eration does not require any special userland program.   Acknowl‐
edgement: Thanks are due to who reverse engineered the EA41x.  In
developing  this  code,  Phil’s userland daemon "etherd", was re‐
ferred to extensively in lieu of accurate documentation  for  the
device.   The  EA41x doesn’t conform to the specification in much
at all.  About the only standard command supported is  Most  com‐
mands  are  6 bytes long, but the recv data is only 1 byte.  Data
must be received by periodically polling the device with the recv
command.
